          "type": "intro",
          "title": "Welcome: The Making of a Mind",
          "content": "Welcome to the first session of our seminar on **Albert Einstein** (1879–1955), arguably the most consequential physicist since Newton.\n\nWe begin not with relativity, not with E=mc², but with a child in a Swabian town who spoke late, played with a compass, and could not abide the bark of a Prussian schoolmaster. Our central question this week:\n\n**How did the cultural, familial, and educational world of late-imperial Germany and Switzerland shape the cognitive habits that would later overturn classical physics?**\n\nWe will work with primary sources: Einstein's own **\"Autobiographical Notes\"** (composed 1946, published 1949 in Schilpp's *Albert Einstein: Philosopher-Scientist*), the **Love Letters** (Renn & Schulmann eds., 1992), and the **Collected Papers of Albert Einstein** (Princeton, 1987–).\n\nKeep one historiographical caution close: Einstein the man and Einstein the legend are not the same person. Our job is to recover the former.",
